<div class="glass-card metallic-border rounded-2xl overflow-hidden carbon-panel my-8 not-prose">
  <div class="steel-header px-6 py-4"><h3 class="font-bold text-white text-lg">2026 update: the federal EV tax credits have ended</h3></div>
  <div class="px-6 py-5 text-sm text-silver-400 leading-relaxed space-y-3">
    <p>Under the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (Public Law 119-21), the federal <strong class="text-silver-200">new clean vehicle credit (30D, up to $7,500)</strong>, the <strong class="text-silver-200">used clean vehicle credit (25E, up to $4,000)</strong> and the <strong class="text-silver-200">commercial clean vehicle credit (45W)</strong> are not available for vehicles acquired after <strong class="text-silver-200">September 30, 2025</strong>. "Acquired" means you signed a written binding contract and made a payment (even a small down payment or a trade-in) on or before that date. If you did, you can still claim the credit when you take delivery.</p>
    <p>The federal home charger credit (30C) does not apply to chargers placed in service after <strong class="text-silver-200">June 30, 2026</strong>. Source: <a class="text-volt-400 underline" href="https://www.irs.gov/clean-vehicle-tax-credits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS: Clean vehicle tax credits</a> and the <a class="text-volt-400 underline" href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/faqs-for-modification-of-sections-25c-25d-25e-30c-30d-45l-45w-and-179d-under-public-law-119-21-139-stat-72-july-4-2025-commonly-known-as-the-one-big-beautiful-bill-obbb" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS OBBB energy credit FAQs</a>.</p>
    <p>In 2026, the money left on the table is mostly <strong class="text-silver-200">state rebates and tax credits, utility rebates, EV charging rates, and automaker or dealer incentives</strong>. Find every program for your ZIP code in the U.S. Department of Energy's <a class="text-volt-400 underline" href="https://afdc.energy.gov/laws/state" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alternative Fuels Data Center state laws and incentives database</a>, and check your electric utility's website for charger rebates and off-peak EV rates.</p>
  </div>
</div>
